\p{The coefficient ring is commutative and unital; the operator
product need not commute. Positive counting pairings and weights enter
in the [real specialization](fdip-0002). Working over a ring first
separates identities of incidence, composition and elimination from
positivity and integration. The general adjoint construction assumes
perfect pairings, and elimination requires an invertible eliminated block;
these hypotheses do not follow from the coefficient ring alone.
Real positivity and measure are additional data for the later Gaussian
laws.}

\transclude{fdip-00AA}

\p{In these terms a field assigns a coefficient to each vertex or edge.
Along an oriented edge, the incidence map subtracts the source value from
the head value. Endpoint pullbacks express this comparison between distinct
field spaces. Composition supplies an operator product on a common field
module; comparing it with pointwise field multiplication gives a discrete
product rule. \ref{fdip-0009} states that rule, and \ref{fdip-00AP} shows
where operator commutativity fails. The alternating four-cycle of
\ref{fdip-0003} is the weighted carrier, with its half-turn.}

